The rumored touchscreen OLED <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> that Apple has in development will adopt the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/iphone/">iPhone</a>'s <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/dynamic-island/">Dynamic Island</a> and a hole-punch camera, reports <em><a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-24/apple-s-touch-screen-macbook-pro-to-have-dynamic-island-new-interface">Bloomberg</a></em>.<br/>
<br/>
<img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-974025" src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/10/Apple-MacBook-Pro-M4-hero.jpg" alt="" width="3840" height="2160" /><br/>
The &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj; will replace the notch, leaving more available screen space for content. As with the &zwnj;iPhone&zwnj;'s &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj;, the Mac &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj; will be interactive and it will contextually expand based on the app or Mac feature in use.<br/>
<br/>
Apple is updating macOS to make it more touch friendly. Users will be able to tap or click on-screen elements, and controls will change based on input method. If a user taps on a menu bar item, for example, it will display a larger set of controls optimized for touch. Touch-based options will be integrated throughout macOS, and it will support <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> features like pinch gestures for zooming in or out and fast scrolling.<br/>
<br/>
The new &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; will be the first Mac that supports touch gestures on the display, despite Apple's insistence over the years that it would not bring touch-based technology to the Mac. Apple did experiment with an OLED Touch Bar in prior &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models, but ultimately removed it after it proved to be unpopular. Apple apparently is not going to advertise the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; as a touch-first device like the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj;, and will let customers use touch and mouse gestures interchangeably for all functions.<br/>
<br/>
Along with a touchscreen and OLED display technology, the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; could have an updated design with a slimmed-down chassis, but it will look a lot like the current &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;. Apple is not removing the keyboard or the trackpad, and sizes aren't expected to change. Apple is planning for OLED versions of both the 14-inch and 16-inch &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models.<br/>
<br/>
